    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The current search for safe repositories for hazardous waste and for petroleum recovery from low permeability reservoirs has stimulated tremendous interest in the flow of fluids through crystalline and tight sedimentary rocks. Since fractures can strongly control the hydraulic behavior of these low permeability formations [e.g. Cacas, et al., 1990; Neuzil, et al., 1984; Tsangand Witherspoon, 1981],attention in this research has been focused on developing a more complete understanding of fluid flow through natural fracture networks. Surprisingly though, few of these studies have taken advantage of the understanding of fracture systems gained from geomechanical investigations during the past few decades.\r\n\r\nThe introduction of geomechanical techniques into hydraulic investigations should have wide spread application. For example, the subsurface distribution of petroleum and other natural resources is typically controlled by the structural and hydraulic environments. Consideration of both the fluid and solid mechanics of a given reservoir should provide more rigorous and detailed site evaluations. Similarly, coupled fluid-solid mathematical models of hydrologic systems should allow for more accurate predictions of contaminant distributions and residence times.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
